== Six Propositions Concerning Satan

And now to prevent Objections, I shall lay down some #emph[Propositions,] or #emph[Conclusions] concerning Satan and his #emph[Devices,] and then give you the Reasons of the Point, and so come to make some Use and Application of the whole to our selves.

#strong[The first #emph[Proposition] or #emph[Conclusion] I shall lay down is this.]

That though Satan hath his #emph[Devices] #footnote[1 Proposition.] to draw souls to sin, yet we must be careful, that we do not lay all our temptations upon Satan, that we do not wrong the Devil, and father that upon him, that is to be fathered upon our own base hearts; I think that oftentimes men charge that upon the Devil, that is to be charged upon their own hearts. #emph[And the Lord said unto the] #footnote[Gen. 3. 13.] #emph[woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, the Serpent beguiled me, and I did eat.] Sin and shifting came into the world together. This is no small baseness of our hearts, that they will be naught, I very naught, and yet will father that naughtiness upon Satan. Man hath an evil root within him; that were there no Devil to tempt him, nor no wicked men in the world to entice him, yet that root of bitterness, that cursed sinful nature that is in him, would draw him to sin, though he knows before hand, #emph[that the wages of sin is eternal death. For out of the heart proceed evil thoughts, murders, adulteries,] #footnote[Rom. 6. ult. Mat. 5. 19.] #emph[fornications, thefts, false witnesses, blasphemies.] The whole frame of man is#footnote[#emph[Cum primum nascimur in omnt continuo pravitate versamur;] we are no sooner borne then buried in a bog of wickednesse, #emph[Tully.]] out of frame, the understanding is dark; the will cross, the memory slippery, the affections crooked, the conscience corrupted, the tongue poisoned, and the heart wholly evil, only evil, and continually evil. Should God chain up Satan, and give him no liberty to tempt, or entice the sons of men to vanity, and folly; yet they would not, yet they could not but sin against him, by reason of that cursed nature that is in them, that will still be a provoking#footnote[Iude 15, 16.] them to those sins, that will provoke and stir up the anger and wrath of God against them. Satan hath only a persuading slight, not an enforcing might; he may tempt us, but without our selves, he cannot conquer us; he#footnote[Iames 1. 4.] may entice us, but without our selves, he cannot hurt us; our hearts carries the greatest stroak in every sin. Satan#footnote[〈 in non-Latin alphabet 〉. The fire is our wood, though it be the Devills flame, #emph[Nazianzen.]] can never undo a man without himself, but a man may easily undo himself without Satan. Satan can only present the golden cup, but he hath no power to force us to drink the poyson that is in the cup; he can only present to us the glory of the world, he cannot force us to fall down and worship him, to enjoy the world, he can only spread his snares; he hath no power to force us to walk in the mid'd of his snares; therefore do the Devil so much right, as not to excuse your selves, by your accusing him, and laying that load upon him, that you should lay upon your own hearts.

#strong[The second #emph[Proposition] is this.]#footnote[2 Proposition.]

That Satan hath a great hand, and stroak in most sins. 'twas Satan that#footnote[Gen. 3. 1. 4, 5.] tempted our first Parents to Rebellion. 'twas Satan that provoked #emph[David] to#footnote[1 Chron. 21. 1.] number the people. 'twas Satan that put #emph[Peter] upon rebuking Christ; therefore saith Christ, #emph[Get thee behind me, Satan.] #footnote[Mat. 16. 22, 23.] 'twas Satan that put #emph[Cain] upon murdering of righteous #emph[Abel:] therefore 'tis that #emph[he is called a murderer from] #footnote[Iohn 8. 44.] #emph[the beginning.] 'twas Satan that put treason into the heart of #emph[Judas,] against Christ. #emph[And supper being ended, the Devil] #footnote[Iohn 13. 2.] #emph[having put it into the heart of Judas Iscatiot, Simons son, to betray him.] 'twas Satan that pu• #emph[Ananias] upon lying: #emph[Peter] said,#footnote[Acts 5. 3.] #emph[Ananias, why hath Satan filled thine heart to lie to the holy Ghost?] As the hand of #emph[Joab] was in the tale of the woman of #emph[Tekoah,] so Satans hand is usually in all the sins that men commit; such is Satans malice against God, and his envy#footnote[#emph[Diaholus tendat deus orobat.] Tertul.] against man, that he will have a hand one way or another in all their finns, though ke knows that all the sins that he provokes others to, shall be charged upon him to his greater woe, and eternal torment. #emph[Ambrose] brings in the Devil boasting against Christ, and chalenging #emph[Judas] as his own; he is not thine Lord Jesus, he is mine; his thoughts beat for me; he eats with thee, but is fed by me; he takes bread from thee, but money from me; he drinks wine with thee, and sells thy blood to me; such is his malice against Christ, and his wrath and rage against man, that he will take all advantages to draw men to that, that may give him advantage to triumph over Christ, and mens souls for ever.

#strong[The third #emph[Proposition] is this.]

That Satan must have a double leave,#footnote[3 Proposition.] before he can do any thing against us; he must have leave from God, and leave from our selves, before he can act any thing against our happiness; he must have his commission from God, as you may see in the example of #emph[Job;] though#footnote[Iob 1. 11, 12. Chap. 2 v 3, 4, 5.] the Devil had malice enough to destroy him, yet he had not so much as power to touch him, till God gave him a Commission.

They could not so much as enter into#footnote[Luke 8. 32.] the swine without leave from Christ; Satan would fain have combated with #emph[Peter;] but this could not he do without leave; #emph[Satan hath desired to have you,] #footnote[Luke 22. 13.] #emph[to winnow you:] So Satan could never have overthrown #emph[Ahab] and #emph[Saul,] but by#footnote[1 Kings 22.] a commission from God; Ah! What a cordial, what a comfort should this be to the Saints, that their greatest, subtilest, and watchfulest enemy cannot hurt, nor harm them without leave from him, who is their sweetest Saviour, their dearest husband, and their choysest friend.

And as Satan must have leave from#footnote[#emph[Adversaria po•e〈…〉 non habet vim c•gendi, sed persuadendi] Is•do•e.] God, so he must have leave of us: when he tempts, we must assent; when he makes offers, we must hearken; when he commands, we must obey, or else all his labour and temptations will be frustrate; and the evil that he tempts us to, shall be put down only to his account; that's a remarkable passage of the Apostle in #emph[Acts 5. 3. Why hath Satan] #footnote[Acts 5. 3.] #emph[filled thy heart to lie to the holy Ghost?] etc.

He doth not expostulate the matter with Satan, he doth not say, Satan, #emph[why] #footnote[They are the wor••, and grossest lyars, who pretend Re•igion, and the Spirit, and yet are acted onley by carnall principles to carnal ends.] #emph[host thou filled Ananias heart to make him lie to the holy Ghost?] But he expostulates the case with #emph[Ananias; Peter] said, #emph[Ananias, why hath Satan filled thine heart to lie to the holy Ghost?] Why hast thou given him an advantage to fill thy heart with infidelity, hypocrisy, and obstinate audacity, to lie to the holy Ghost? As If he had said, Satan could never have done this in thee (which will now for ever undo thee) unless thou hadst first given him leave. If when a temptation comes, a man cries out, and saith; ah Lord! Here is a temptation that would force me, that would deflowr my soul, and I have no strength to with-stand it; oh! Help, help, for thy honours sake, for thy Sons sake, for thy promise sake, 'tis a sign that Satan hath not gained your consent, but committed a rape upon your souls, which he shall dearly pay for.

#strong[The fourth Proposition is this.]

That no weapons, but spiritual#footnote[4 Proposition.] weapons will be useful and serviceable to the soul in fighting, and combating with the Devil; this the Apostle shows, #emph[Wherefore take unto you] (saith#footnote[Ephes. 6. 13.] he) #emph[the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand in the evil day, and having done all, to stand.] So the same Apostle tells you, #emph[that the weapons of your warfare are not carnal, but mighty through God, to] #footnote[2 Cor. 10. 4.] #emph[the casting down of strong bolds;] you have not to do with a weak, but with a mighty#footnote[We read of many that out of greatnesse of spirit could offer violence to nature, but were at a loss when they come to deale with a corruption, or a temptation.] enemy; and therefore you had need look to it, that your weapons are mighty, and that they cannot be, unless they are spiritual; carnal weapons have no might, nor spirit in them towards the making of a conquest upon Satan. 'twas not #emph[Davids] sling, nor stone that gave him the honor, & advantage of setting his feet upon #emph[Goliah,] but his faith in the name of the Lord of Hosts. #emph[Thou comest] #footnote[1 Sam. 17. 45.] #emph[to me with a sword, with a spear, and with a shield: but I am come to thee in the name of the Lord of Hosts, the God of the Armies of Israel, whom thou hast defied.] He that#footnote[#emph[Heraclius] his #emph[Motto] was #emph[(a Deo victoria)] it is God that gives victories, and that should be every Christians Motto.)] fights against Satan, in the strength of his own resolutions, constitution, or education, will certainely fly and fall before him; Satan will be too hard for such a soul, and lead him captive at his pleasure. The only way to stand, conquer, and triumph, is still to plead, 'tis written, as Christ did. There is no sword, but the two-edged sword of the#footnote[Mat. 4. 10.] Spirit; that will be found to be mettle of proof, when a soul comes to engage against Satan: Therefore when you are tempted to uncleanness, plead, #emph['tis] written, #emph[Be ye holy as I am holy. And] #footnote[1 Pet. 15. 16• 2 Cor. 5. 7. Chap. 1.] #emph[l•t us cleanse our selves from all filthiness of the flesh and spirit, perfecting holiness in the •eare of the Lord.] If he tempts• you to distrust Gods providence, and fatherly care of you, plead, 'tis written, #emph[They] #footnote[Psal. 34. 9.] #emph[that fear the Lord shall want nothing that is good.] 'tis written, #emph[The Lord will give] #footnote[Psal. 84. 11.] #emph[grace and glory, and no good thing will he with-hold from them that purlely live.] If he tempt you to fear, that you shall faint, and fall, and never be able to run to the end of the race that is set before#footnote[Iob 17. 9.] you, plead, 'tis written, #emph[The righteous shall hold on his way, and he that hath clean hands shall be stronger, and stronger.] 'tis written, #emph[I will make an everlasting covevenant with them, that I will not turn away] #footnote[Ier. 32. 40.] #emph[from them, to do them good, but I will put my fear in their hearts, that they shall not depart from me.] 'tis written, #emph[They that wait upon the Lord, they shall renew their] #footnote[Isa. 40. 31.] #emph[strength: they shall mount up with wings as Eagles, they shall run, and not be weary, and they shall walk, and not •aint.] If Satan tempt you to think, that because your sun for the present is set in a cloud, that therefore 'twill rise no more, and that the face of God will shine no more upon you? That your best days are now at an end, and that you must spend all your time in sorrow, and fighing? Plead, 'tis written, #emph[He will turn again, he] #footnote[Mica. 7. 19.] #emph[will have compassion upon us, and cast all our sins into the depths of the sea.] 'tis written, #emph[For a small moment have I forsaken] #footnote[Isa. 54. 7.] #emph[thee, but with great mercies will I gather] #footnote[Verse 8.] #emph[thee. In a little wrath I hid my face from thee for a moment, but with everlasting kindness will I have mercy on thee, saith the Lord thy Redeemer.] 'tis written, #emph[The mountains shall depart, and the hills be] #footnote[Verse 10.] #emph[removed, but my kindness shall not depart from thee, neither shall the Covenant of my peace be removed, saith the Lord, that hath mercy on thee.] 'tis written, #emph[Can a woman] #footnote[Isa. 49. 15.] #emph[forget her sucking child, that she should not have compassion on the son of her womb? Yea, they may forget, yet will not I forget thee. Behold, I have graven thee upon the] #footnote[Verse 16.] #emph[palms of my bands, thy walls are continually before me.] If ever you would be too hard for Satan, and after all your assaults have your bow abide in strength, then take to you the word of God, #emph[which is the two-edged sword of the Spirit,] #footnote[Ephes. 6. 1.] #emph[and the shield of faith, whereby you shall be able to quench all the fiery darts of the Devil.] 'tis not spitting at Satans name, nor crossing your selves, nor leaning to your own resolutions, that will get you the victory.

#emph[Luther] reports of #emph[Staupicius] a #emph[German] Minister, that he acknowledged himself, that before he came to understand aright the free and powerful grace of God, that he vowed, and resolved an hundred times against some particular sin, and never could get power over it; at last he saw the reason, to be his trusting to his own resolution; therefore be skilful in the word of righteousness, and in the actings of faith upon Christ, and his victory, and that Crown of glory that is set before you, and Satan will certainly #emph[fly from you,] #footnote[Iames 4. 7.] #emph[etc.]

#strong[The fifth #emph[Proposition] is this.]

That we may read much of Satans#footnote[5 Proposition.] nature, and disposition, by the divers names and Epithites, that are given him in the Scriptures. Sometimes he is called #emph[Behemoth,] which is #emph[bruta,] whereby#footnote[Iob 40. 15.] the greatness, and brutishness of the Devil is figured. Those evil spirits are sometimes called 〈 in non-Latin alphabet 〉, Accusers, for their calumnies and slanders; and 〈 in non-Latin alphabet 〉 evil ones for their malice: Satan is #emph[Adversarius,] an adversary that troubleth#footnote[Revel 9.] and molesteth: #emph[Abaddon] is a destroyer. They are called #emph[Tempters] for their suggestion, #emph[Lions] for their devouring, #emph[Dragons] for their cruelty, and #emph[Serpents] for their subtlety, etc. As his names are, so is he; as face answers to face, so do Satans names answer to his nature; he hath the worst names, and the worst nature of all created creatures, etc.

#strong[The sixth and last #emph[Proposition] is this.]

That God will shortly tread down#footnote[#emph[6 Proposition. Rom. 16. 20. 〈 in non-Latin alphabet 〉 suntripsei] from #emph[suntribo,] the Greek word signifies to break, or crush a thing to pieces, being applyed to the feet; it noteth that breaking or crushing, which is by stamping upon a thing.] Satan under the Saints feet. Christ our Champion hath already won the Field, and will shortly set our feet upon the necks of our spiritual enemies. Satan is a foiled Adversary, Christ hath led him captive, and triumph't over him upon the Cross. Christ hath already overcome him, and put weapons into your hands, that you may overcome him also, & set your feet upon his neck. Though Satan be a roaring Lion, yet #emph[Christ, who is the Lion of the Tribe of Judah] will make Satan fly, and fall before you. Let Satan do his worst, yet you shall have the honour and the happiness to triumph over him. Cheer up, you precious sons of #emph[Sion,] for the certainty and sweetness of Victory will abundantly recompense you, for all the pains you have taken, in making resistance against Satans temptations. The broken horns of Satan shall be Trumpets of our triumph, and the Cornets of our joy, etc.
